\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For the full nonrelativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian with two electron spin states, M fixed nuclei of arbitrary positions and charges $Z_j\geq 1$ and total $Z=sum$ $Z_j$, is strict n-electron binding possible only for $n\leq Z+CM$ for one universal constant C independent of charges and positions? Also ask that adding electrons above that threshold does not lower the ground energy.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For the full nonrelativistic Coulomb Hamiltonian with two electron spin states, M fixed nuclei of arbitrary positions and charges $Z_j\geq 1$ and total $Z=sum$ $Z_j$, is strict n-electron binding possible only for $n\leq Z+CM$ for one universal constant C independent of charges and positions? Also ask that adding electrons above that threshold does not lower the ground energy.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Uniform-excess-charge-for-Coulomb-molecules-and-the-outer-radius-of-neutral-atoms-September-24-2026/build/sections/01-introduction.tex}.
